\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Effective conductance}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let the \vocabk{Gaussian induced action}{fdip-005R} have
strictly positive edge weights, and let the graph be connected.
\ref{fdip-005U} inverts #{L_w} on the complement of the constant
fields and sets #{L_w^+} to zero on constants. Let #{h} be the
counting inner product on vertex functions. For distinct
vertices #{a} and #{b}, the \newvocab{effective conductance}
between them is
##{c(a,b)=\bigl(h(\delta_a-\delta_b,
L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b))\bigr)^{-1}.}
When #{a} and #{b} are the endpoints of an edge #{e}, this is
the reciprocal of the effective resistance of #{e}.}

\p{Let #{K} be a nonempty set of vertices, and let the energy of
a function #{x} be #{h(x,L_w x)}. A function on the whole vertex
set extends a function #{u} on #{K} when the two agree on #{K}.
Write #{U} for the vertices outside #{K}, and let #{i_U} be the
inclusion of functions supported on #{U}. The compression off
#{K} is #{i_U^* L_w i_U}. It is not the restriction of an
arbitrary function, which does not name that projection. If
#{K} is the whole vertex set, or if this compression is positive
definite, the \newvocab{kept form} of #{u} is the minimum of
the energy over extensions of #{u}.}
